    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "N6-methyl-adenosine (m6A) is a posttranscriptional modification to RNAs that is critical to the development of many eukaryotic organisms. The identification of both methylases and demethylases suggests that cells can actively control m6A levels to regulate mRNA metabolism. However, the regulatory mechanism of m6A RNA methylation is not well understood. In this study, we aim to understand how the microRNA pathway may regulate RNA methylation. Using qRT-PCR and HPLC, we observe that in knock outs of Dicer and DGCR8, while global mRNA methylation levels remain largely unchanged, methylation in the transcripts of multiple pluripotency genes in mouse embryonic stem cells (mESCs) is reduced. To understand the mechanism by which miRNAs affect mRNA methylation, we determine that the protein and gene expression of methylases, demethylases, and writers remain unchanged following knock out of Dicer, DGCR8, and Argonautes. We have performed m6A-IP seq from Dicer and DGCR8 knockout cells, and we hypothesize that identifying the transcripts that are preferentially methylated or demethylated by DGCR8, Dicer, and Argonautes will allow us to understand how methylation of RNA is regulated by the miRNA pathway. Overall, these results are critical in elucidating the role and regulation of m6A RNA methylation in mammalian embryonic stem cells.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
